e.\" [794] As a result, there was nothing special found in the room with the horse. This was a rather disappointing fact, but at the same time, the detectives seemed to have breathed a sigh of relief. Perhaps it would have been even worse if anything else had happened. [795] Later, the police began taking statements in a room on the third floor. This room was usually used as a guest room. Although it was large enough to be considered a guest room due to the size of the mansion, the interior layout was exactly the same as the rooms on the second floor. [796] Akiyoshi, Yan, and the housekeeper, along with the other staff and servants, were questioned. The detectives were determined to make up for their previous lack of progress, and they listened intently, afraid to miss a single word or phrase. [797] However, after three hours, everything ended in vain. There were no significant findings, and little progress was made in the investigation. [798] Perhaps everyone knew this would be the outcome from the start. The killer was bold and meticulous\u2014according to Mukai, such a person would not leave any clues. The police's inquiries yielded mostly negative responses, with occasional affirmations that only confirmed known facts. [799] To summarize the results: from 3:00 to 4:00 a.m., the time of the crime, no one had an alibi. Furthermore, there were no new discoveries regarding the motive for the bizarre actions of the victim or the reason for the double murder. Of course, the police would not rely solely on surface statements, but it was also a fact that the detective's punch did not elicit a reaction. [800] The detective continued to ask repetitive questions while observing the reactions of the others. However, this tactic also proved ineffective. Perhaps it was because the detective had not yet fully grasped the characteristics of each individual. [801] The members of the Yoshinobu family (although not all of them) were like Sakura, with a reserved personality. In a more positive light, this could be described as calmness, which seems to have led them to a state of indifference. They lacked the clich\u00e9d histrionics and also the urgency that should accompany the death of a family member. This group reacted in a detached manner, almost like a group of opium addicts. [802] Even if it wasn't the Inspector, anyone would become frustrated with this extreme impatience. When all the statements were recorded, the Inspector collapsed, exhausted, onto the back of his chair. [803] As for what the police gained from this fruitless dialogue, it was the testimony of the housekeeper, Yoshiko. [804] Yoshiko gave two important testimonies. One was about a letter. Last night, when she delivered dinner to Yoshinobu's room, she saw him writing on a note, which could easily be the letter to the detective in the town of Higashiyama. [805] The Inspector read the letter, which was similar to the one sent to the Inspector but did not contain any details about the case. However, it was quite peculiar that Yoshinobu wanted to hire another detective after the Inspector had decided to come to the Kurogane Castle. [806] The other one - actually more significant - further aggravated the detective's disappointment. It was related to the key of the \"Hell Gate\". [807] As previously mentioned, the room with the horse corpse, the \"Hell Gate\", is located in the tower, surrounded by thick stone walls on all sides except for the door. There are no windows, only several arrow holes about 8 meters high. [808] When the body was found, the door with the hell pattern was locked, and the \"Hell Gate\" was completely sealed. There are two keys to the door, one original and one spare. The original key was held in the hand of the corpse of the horse. Initially, everyone thought that the spare key, which was kept by Yasu, was used to lock the \"Hell Gate\", but this view was rejected through this record. [809] \"The spare key and other spare keys were kept in a wall-mounted safe, and it has not been used for several years. Until I opened the safe at the request of the detective, the surface of the safe was covered with a uniform layer of thin dust. If someone had opened it recently, there would have been fingerprints.\" [810] The housekeeper spoke confidently. No matter how the detective tried to question her, the answer remained the same. Moreover, the more she repeated the question, the more convinced she became. [811] \"I won,\" said Kumotani, but he didn't seem too happy. His expression was like that of an A-grade student who got 98 points. [812] \"You are right. You are always right.\" [813] \"Provided that the housekeeper's testimony is correct.\" [814] Kumotani stopped playing the game of picking the beads. Now there were five stars between his hands. Kumotani often played the game of picking the beads to be able to concentrate. [815] \"You mean she's lying?\" [816] \"I'm not saying she's giving false testimony. However, from the perspective of 'possibility', there are still some chances of 'mistakes', right? But, I bet 125,000 yen that she's not wrong.\" [817] 125,000 yen is the monthly rent of Mukai's apartment. [818] Unusually, Mukai didn't interrupt during the questioning. He usually asked a couple of unexpected questions, but this time he was busily moving his fingers around the detective. As an observer, I couldn't help but wonder if he was even listening to what others were saying. [819] \"Are you going to start a bet?\" [820] \"Maybe you can even bet on a million-dollar ticket,\" Mukai smiled. [821] \"How can you be so sure?\" [822] \"I just have a knack for it. Based on the provocative, or you could say mocking, attitude the killer has shown so far, it wouldn't be surprising if there was a locked room.\" [823] \"Doesn't that provocative attitude include the possibility of using a third key?\" [824] \"An outsider would have a hard time replicating that key.\" [825] Horikoshi nodded repeatedly. This small point was something that Jyuuchi could have easily understood. However, his hesitation prevented him from admitting it from the bottom of his heart. [826] \"An expert could probably replicate it, but that would leave traces. Unless the killer has a friend in the business.\" [827] \"I know the killer doesn't have any dangerous accomplices!\" The detective reluctantly conceded, \"Anyway, the lock was on the outside, right?\" [828] \"If the killer couldn't escape from the room, then it was definitely locked from the outside. That place was like a stone prison. There are many ways to do it.\" [829] \"Many ways, such as?\" [830] \"Charm, telekinesis... But I think you understand it won't be those clich\u00e9d methods.\" [831] \"Is it about pride?\" [832] \"These days, lines and ropes are no longer fashionable.\" [833] \"This kind of guy should have been eliminated earlier!\" [834] \"Don't get too impatient. We still have unexplored areas.\" [835] Regarding the locked room, Kumotsubo didn't seem to have any specific ideas. However, his calm demeanor suggested that he might have found a way to approach the problem. [836] \"By the way, Detective Jotaro,\" Kumagai changed the subject, \"Have you heard of 'Love of Three Oranges'?\" [837] \"I don't know... Detective Kodama, do you know?\" [838] Detective Kodama nodded, a bit reserved: \"It's a Prokofiev opera. I remember it was later arranged into a song cycle.\" [839] \"Love of Three Oranges is a masterpiece written by Soviet composer Prokofiev during his escape. He was not yet thirty years old at the time, and this work, along with 'Lieutenant Kije,' has become his signature piece.\" [840] \"I've only heard of 'Peter and the Wolf.'\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:28 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWhen Ito's head was cut off, the brain cells were temporarily paralyzed due to the low temperature. When the neural fibers of Utsugi were combined with them, the temperature difference between the neural fibers caused the production of an electromotive force. This electromotive force became the electric potential for neural activity, which activated the retracting nerves in a state of apparent death. In other words, Ito's nerves and Utsugi's nerves were indeed connected. [3969] \"Now, let's talk about muscles. Modern medicine shows that muscles can be repaired, and muscles that have just been cut and have not yet oxidized can be connected together. Although they cannot make large movements, they can still temporarily remain connected due to their adhesiveness. [3970] \"Connecting a person who has died once?\" [3971] \"No, strictly speaking, Itou has not yet died. I'm not sure what the death criteria for humans are, but even brain death cannot occur in less than zero point zero one seconds. Yes, the head and body cut off are still alive. As for whether it can be called the first death, this question should be considered by the gods. [3972] \"However, this anomaly created a monster with Utsugi's body - Dr. Frankenstein. At this moment, the blood vessels and trachea are not important. This artificial person only needs to survive for a short time. [3973] \"A short time?\" The chief inspector asked. [3974] \"Yes, because Itou only needs a few minutes. Now I will explain the significance of this. [3975] \"Mutsu took a deep breath and showed a hesitant expression on his face.\" [3976] \"In short, It\u00f4 received a new body from Utsunomiya and opened his eyes again due to this shock. Of course, the most surprised person was Maki. She thought she had killed someone - and even switched bodies - and suddenly opened her eyes and stood up. [3977] \"This is a horror movie. I can't believe it. [3978] \"So, what did It\u00f4 see when he woke up due to the hand of the gods? Yes, what he saw was Maki, who was standing there holding a knife. And It\u00f4 remembered. His memories at the moment of death - he woke up from a coma due to the shock of having his head cut off, and now he saw himself about to be killed. [3979] \"Due to extreme fear, It\u00f4 ran out of the room, desperately trying to escape into the 'Gate of Hell.' He grabbed the key that was placed beside him. Yes, we thought Utsunomiya had escaped into the Gate of Hell, which is why it felt unnatural. But if we think that It\u00f4 ran into the Gate of Hell to escape, it makes perfect sense. After all, Utsunomiya's body was being controlled by It\u00f4's consciousness. [3980] \"Of course, It\u00f4 probably wanted to cry for help. But it was like someone whose throat had been cut, who could only make a horrible, gurgling sound. At that time, it was unclear to what extent It\u00f4's consciousness was awake, but he instinctively fled to the 'Gate of Hell.' Then, when It\u00f4 reached the 'Gate of Hell,' he naturally locked it from the inside. [3981] \"His body belonged to Ushio, but his head - his spirit belonged to Itou. Itou is left-handed, so he locked the door with his left hand as usual. That's why the left hand of the right-handed Ushio spasmed due to the abrupt twisting movement he was not used to.\" [3982] \"However, at this point, Itou was already exhausted. Although the nerve fibers were connected, a person whose muscles and blood vessels were cut off could not live long. After Itou locked the door, he fell down naturally, holding the key with his left hand.\" [3983] \"This is the reason why Ushio's left hand was holding the key.\" [3984] \"When Itou fell down, the head separated from Ushio's body and became a mere head and corpse. As life stopped, blood flowed out, creating the illusion that the 'Gate of Hell' was the scene of the crime.\" [3985] \"This is the solution to the locked room. The 'Gate of Hell' is a complete locked room. As for the locksmith, I have no intention of hiding it. It was the hand of the god.\" [3986] The police chief still looked incredulous. I was the 