stuho revised the plan, hastily removed Y\u016ben, who had no blood ties, from the list, and killed McArthur instead. At this point, the choice of theme was simple. Since he was wearing a top hat, there was only one possible theme: 'The Mystery of the Roman Hat.' [4023] \"By the way, Y\u016ben always wore a lace cap, so she would have been killed using the theme of 'The Mystery of the Roman Hat' originally.\" [4024] So, if it wasn't for McArthur, the head on the table would have been Y\u016ben's with a lace cap on, right? [4025] \"Suguru was left for 'The Mystery of the Egyptian Cross,' so Y\u016ben's 'The Mystery of the American Gun' was also decided. As you can see, Mistuho's plan was flexible and adaptable, allowing her to achieve the best outcome from all situations.\" [4026] \"Mr. McCarto's reasoning yesterday was probably nonsense. Since he had teamed up with the fog drawing, he had to come up with a suitable answer. He said so, didn't he? Because I'm the killer, I have to leave this house to avoid getting too involved. [4027] \"However, this was actually about Mr. McCarto himself. It was Mr. McCarto who got too involved. Even after his mother was killed, he still cooperated with the fog drawing. As a result, he ended up losing his life. [4028] \"I thought of Akutagawa's 'Tsuchigumo'. Tsuchigumo couldn't bear to see his parents suffering in hell and shouted out loud. Unexpectedly, Tsuchigumo was saved by this action. However, Mr. McCarto watched his mother being killed - even though he didn't know about it beforehand - and took on the guilt himself. [4029] \"It's more like the story in 'The Spider Silk', isn't it? [4030] \"Why did the fog drawing do this?\" The detective murmured dejectedly.\" [4031] \"The motive... it's a twisted Oedipus complex.\" [4032] Wakisugi pointed to the shining corpse of Kanayan with his right hand. [4033] \"What does it look like?\" [4034] Wakisugi, who questioned like a rhetorician, was ultimately a failure. [4035] He gave the answer himself. [4036] \"It's a 'god.' The only savior who descended to this world\u2026\n[4037] \"This is what Fujiko truly pursued. She loved her father Kanayanagi. She didn't hate the Kanayanagi who abandoned her and her mother, but instead constantly missed him from afar in a distant foreign land. It's probably the influence of her mother, who was deeply in love with Kanayanagi, that led her to believe he was her savior.\" [4038] \"Kanayanagi?\" [4039] \"Yes. But reality is different. The Kanayanagi who came to meet Fujiko was just a weak and powerless ordinary citizen. Recognizing this, Fujiko's disappointment must have been immeasurable. After twenty years of accumulated expectations, this tragic fact was equivalent to the collapse of her value system and her desire for redemption. [4040] \"In a normal situation, Fujiko would have given up her illusions about Kanayanagi\u2014no, her faith. But she pursued something else. She pursued 'god.' And she inherited the bloodline of the Mimura family.\" [4041] \"Mistuho would do anything to make Kaji into 'God'. If the reality of him was too disappointing, she would create a new 'God' herself. A new Christ\u2026\"\n[4042] \"\u2026\"\n[4043] \"At this point, Mistuho changed her role from a humble believer like Peter to the Virgin Mary. In order to make Kaji into 'God', she had to eliminate other members of the Mikumo family, making him the only 'God' and giving him all the power of the Mikumo family. With both 'blood' and 'power', the great father Kaji that Mistuho envisioned could be born. [4044] \"She was insane. But she held onto the lofty ideals of a fanatical believer and charged towards her own peak. She created her own aesthetics and overcame all obstacles. [4045] \"And now, Kaji has become 'God'.\" [4046] \"Of course, not the living Kanou. The living Kanou was insignificant. The 'Kanou' filled with Mistuho's ideas had to be a mere collection of facts, an entity beyond all, a god symbolized. [4047] \"Therefore, Kanou