re not 'clear facts.'\" [2538] \"Are you saying we can't rely on any of these phenomena?\" [2539] Houdou wanted to say that if everything was a killer's diversion, the case would lose its foundation. [2540] \"I didn't say that. Underneath the surface phenomena that the killer has revealed to us, there are usually deeper phenomena. The killer does not want us to notice these 'underlying phenomena.' On the contrary, to reach the truth, we must find them.\" [2541] \"Are you referring to the work you often do, piecing together unexpected connections?\" [2542] \"The result is unexpected, not the process... Well, whatever.\" [2543] Mutsu reached into his pocket, realizing he had given the thread to the twins, and placed his idle hands on his knees. [2544] \"What are these underlying phenomena... Please think about it. There is a fact that has never been surfaceized, but is very distinctive.\" [2545] The detective leaned forward slightly. [2546] \"So far we have seen four corpses. What's strange is that they were all found intact. The killer cut off the head or foot, but didn't hide any of them. Of course, Ito's head was in a very hard-to-find place, but it was also guessed by the rice paddy. It can be seen that the killer's purpose is not to hide the body, but even shows an active attitude towards our discovery.\" [2547] \"...\"\n[2548] Everyone seemed to be confused, including me. I looked at Mutsuji, hoping he would give an answer. [2549] \"Decapitation usually means the replacement of the victim and the perpetrator. Of course, there are many exceptions, such as this time. To put it more clearly, in this case, decapitation itself is the purpose. If the killer had hidden the head, what would we think? We would naturally think that the killer needed to hide the head to implement some kind of replacement trick, so he decapitated it.\" [2550] \"Hmm.\" [2551] Jyugo chimed in, but his face still showed a look of dissatisfaction. [2552] \"If the purpose of decapitation could directly or indirectly point to the killer's identity, the killer would have hidden the head completely to blur the purpose of decapitation. Or they might have constantly reminded us of the formula 'decapitation = replacement,' causing our investigation to stagnate. However, the killer did not do this.\" [2553] \"They just didn't think about it and didn't hide the head,\" I interjected. [2554] \"This killer is not that foolish. His actions far exceed our imagination. It might have been an accident a few times, but with four bodies being found in a deliberately arranged manner with a dramatic flair, it is no longer an accident. It should be considered the direct intention of the killer.\" [2555] Kurokawa argued vehemently, as if defending his opponent, the killer. [2556] \"I understand all this. What's next?\" [2557] \"Okay, Detective Juch\u016b.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."